nting, trade and war trail (Great Minquas / Conestoga Path)","unverified_note":"Opening dates by segment not confirmed; encyclopedia pages read only through search summaries (NCpedia returned 403)","source_url":"https://scencyclopedia.org/sce/?p=6725","todo":"hand-trace","clipped_to_extent":false},"type":null},{"properties":{"id":"R7-02","kind":"road","name":"Forbes Road","year_open":1758,"date_quality":"partial","source":"R7 (roads_R7.csv)","reference":"Hulbert 1903","kind_as_r7":"military road","year_basis":"r7_year_open","year_open_r7":1758,"year_note":"Cut as a military road in 1758; settler route after 1763. Upgrade as Pennsylvania State Road 1791 rests on one source","other_names":"Pennsylvania State Road; Glade Road","endpoint_a":"Carlisle PA","endpoint_b":"Pittsburgh (Fort Pitt) PA","unverified_note":"1791 state-road date unverified (single source); Hulbert not opened in this pass (Gutenberg unavailable)","source_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/41118","todo":"hand-trace","clipped_to_extent":false},"type":null},{"properties":{"id":"R7-03","kind":"road","name":"Braddock's Road","year_open":1755,"date_quality":"unverified","source":"R7 (roads_R7.csv)","reference":"Wikipedia n.d. (lead only); Armao n.d.","kind_as_r7":"military road","year_basis":"r7_year_open","year_open_r7":1755,"year_note":"Cut in 1755; stopped short of Fort Duquesne after Braddock's defeat","endpoint_a":"Cumberland MD (Fort Cumberland)","endpoint_b":"Monongahela River near Pittsburgh PA","native_path_basis":"Washington's 1754 road (its predecessor) followed the Nemacolin Trail (Armao)","unverified_note":"Only encyclopedic summaries seen; endpoints and later use need a primary or NPS source","source_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forbes_Road","todo":"hand-trace","clipped_to_extent":false},"type":null},{"properties":{"id":"R7-04","kind":"road","name":"Crown Point Road","year_open":1760,"date_quality":"partial","source":"R7 (roads_R7.csv)","reference":"Crown Point Road Association n.